  "intent_snapshot": {
    "contract": "assurance_kernel/task_intent/v1",
    "task_id": "2026-09-06-003-claude-host-resolve-finding",
    "goal": "Expose the Kernel's existing resolve_finding operation on the Claude Code Host MCP surface as defined by docs/specs/claude-host-resolve-finding.spec.md: one additive ordinary tool that carries task_id and finding_id into the existing ordinary-operation port, so a Claude Host imm-loop that has fixed and verified a finding's cause can clear it and let the Kernel reproject the next obligation, instead of terminating fail-closed on 'Kernel requires resolve_findings' with no reachable operation. This task_id succeeds the stopped 2026-09-06-001 and 2026-09-06-002 canaries of the same scope, neither of which could clear its own findings on a Host that does not yet publish resolve_finding; their terminal records are preserved under .imm/audit/. The batch runtime source that 002's Review found in the checked-in bundle without a committed origin is now committed, so this enrollment's base_head carries source for every line that bundle contains.",
    "acceptance": [
      {
        "id": "acc-claude-resolve-finding-tool",
        "assertion": "listMcpTools() exposes a resolve_finding tool whose inputSchema declares both task_id and finding_id and requires exactly [\"task_id\", \"finding_id\"], and whose annotations are the ordinary non-privileged shape rather than privilegedAnnotations(), because canary_application.ts builds the Kernel action without a capability and the Pi Host imm-canary-work.ts already lists resolve_finding in its ordinary KERNEL_OPERATIONS. Calling the tool through createMcpRuntime on a fixture repository whose TaskRecord holds an open blocking finding resolves exactly that finding in the persisted TaskRecord, appends exactly one history entry, and moves the Kernel projection's next_obligation off resolve_findings; the assertion reads the resulting record and projection rather than only the tool listing, so a tool registered without working dispatch fails.",
        "verification": "{\n  \"contract\": \"assurance_kernel/verification_descriptor/v1\",\n  \"runner_id\": \"bun\",\n  \"runner_version\": \"1.3.14\",\n  \"argv\": [\n    \"test\",\n    \"tests/claude-host-authority.test.ts\"\n  ],\n  \"cwd\": \".\",\n  \"timeout_ms\": 180000,\n  \"max_output_bytes\": 65536\n}\n"
      },
      {
        "id": "acc-claude-resolve-finding-fail-closed",
        "assertion": "Every rejection path stays fail-closed with a byte-identical TaskRecord and zero Kernel writes: a missing or non-string finding_id is rejected by the adapter before any Kernel call, and an unknown finding id, an already-resolved finding, a non-active lifecycle, and the unresolved_user_decision and replan_required kinds each surface the reducer's KernelInvariantError unchanged, proving the adapter reads no findings, tests no kind, and adds no second authority. Byte-identical zero-write verification covers the TaskRecord, workspace, and active claim bytes and file identities. A connection with no bound host version and a connection that never negotiated interactive elicitation both reject the tool exactly as they reject advance_assurance. PRIVILEGED_OPERATIONS is unchanged, and the eight pre-existing tools keep their exact order, schemas, annotations, and dispatch. The checked-in plugins/immune-brain/dist/claude/mcp-server.mjs matches a fresh generate so the published surface cannot drift from its runtime source.",
        "verification": "{\n  \"contract\": \"assurance_kernel/verification_descriptor/v1\",\n  \"runner_id\": \"bun\",\n  \"runner_version\": \"1.3.14\",\n  \"argv\": [\n    \"test\",\n    \"tests/claude-host-authority.test.ts\",\n    \"tests/claude-host-package.test.ts\"\n  ],\n  \"cwd\": \".\",\n  \"timeout_ms\": 240000,\n  \"max_output_bytes\": 65536\n}\n"
      }
    ],
